  "joint_backoff_gate_bits": 97,
  "joint_presence_bit_was_already_false": true,
  "joint_presence_gate_bits": 97,
  "per_factor_gate_bits": 57,
  "per_factor_gate_bytes": 8,
  "statement": "THREE measured facts, not one. (1) A per-factor gate has no bit whose meaning is 'this conjunction', so the conjunction deletion changes ZERO bits at any size. (2) A joint PRESENCE gate has ONE bit that means both 'never seen' and 'deleted', so on this split the deletion is ALSO a no-op on it -- the bit was already false -- while it refuses every unseen conjunction, which is measured on the scored corners. (3) The cheapest object that expresses the deletion WITHOUT refusing the unseen is a SEPARATE deleted-pair array beside the evidence, which is exactly what `coupling_hole` is; the generous `joint_backoff` rival is given one and is expected to tie the field store on the refusal half, declared before it was scored.",
